- op: discuss  # d82f
  at: '2026-09-17T23:32:28Z'  # d82f
  by: agent  # d82f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d82f
  clock: 6  # d82f
  nonce: d82f  # d82f
  message: "CORRECTION FIRST, THEN CLOSURE.\n\nCORRECTING MY OWN NOTE FROM EARLIER TODAY. I wrote that this row's\n\"zero call edges differ\" result was \"SUPERSEDED by fixture scope\" because the\nbackend contributes 637 edges on a real crate. THAT WAS WRONG, and it was\nwrong in the flattering direction: I counted edges without checking their\nTYPE. All 637 are `references`. ZERO are call-family.\n\nTHE ARM-VS-ARM MEASUREMENT THIS ROW DEMANDED, now run on a real crate\n(~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns, 16 .rs, at 1aa5b46b1b):\n\n                       arm A (default)   arm B (backend on)\n    nodes                      581             1255\n    edges                      797             1550\n    CALL-FAMILY EDGES          453              453\n    call edges only in B                          0\n    call edges only in A                          0\n    receiver shapes    qualified 24      qualified 24\n                       typed_var  7      typed_var  7\n    validation violations        0                2\n\nSO THIS ROW'S HEADLINE REPRODUCES EXACTLY AT REAL-CRATE SCALE. Not \"few\" --\nZERO call-family edges differ, and not one receiver is typed differently. The\nbackend doubles the node count and nearly doubles the edge count while\nchanging nothing a consumer asks a call graph for. What it adds is 637\n`references` edges and 669 nodes of which 500 are `local`.\n\nThat directly sustains this row's caution about WI-nanom: \"already shipped for\nrust\" is NOT evidence that a fidelity backend converts into typed receivers.\nOn the one real crate now measured, it converts into zero.\n\nWHAT THIS ROW FILED AS ITS CORE IS FIXED. Per its own 2026-08-23 self-\ncorrection (\"drop the dedup half to WI-gojum, KEEP THE origin_run_id\nVIOLATIONS AS THIS ITEM\"): those are gone. 1306 -> 0. Root cause and fix on\nWI-didag (the success path returned run=None and the translator fabricated a\nthrowaway AnalysisRun to satisfy Edge's guard). Verified on this same arm-B\nrun: 0 dangling FKs among 669 nodes and 637 edges.\n\nTHE DEDUP HALF IS NOT A DEFECT, per this row's own correction and WI-gojum:\nthe SCIP backend runs alongside tree-sitter BY DESIGN (ADR-0012 coexistence,\nSteps 2-3 parked by the owner 2026-07-19), and stable-id parity is readiness\nfor a future dedup, not a dedup that runs. Duplicates are expected. The\nreadiness observation is surfaced to WI-gojum rather than folded here.\n\nTHE 2 REMAINING VIOLATIONS ARE A DIFFERENT AND LARGER DEFECT, filed separately\nrather than absorbed: arm B introduces a 39.7% stable_id collision (498 of\n1255 Symbols share an id) and degenerate fingerprints, because the importer\nmints a Symbol per SCIP `local` binding with the SCIP local id as the NAME --\n500 symbols called \"1\", \"3\", \"17\", \"102\". arm A has zero violations. Filed as\nits own row; it needs a decision about whether locals belong in a behavior map\nat all, which is not this row's question.\n\nSTATUS: done on the filed core, with the arm-vs-arm measurement the row asked\nfor and did not have."  # d82f
